1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,017
Thank you. I want to spend the next twenty minutes on a

2
00:00:04,067 --> 00:00:08,084
question that sounds simple and turns out not to be: who governs

3
00:00:08,134 --> 00:00:12,151
the robots? Not the chatbots. Not the recommendation engines. The machines. The

4
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:16,218
drone over the stadium, the car with nobody at the wheel, the

5
00:00:16,268 --> 00:00:20,285
arm on the factory floor, and, soon, the robot in your parent's

6
00:00:20,335 --> 00:00:24,352
care home. We went looking for every rule in the world that

7
00:00:24,402 --> 00:00:28,420
reaches those machines, and for every paper and strategy written about them.

8
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:32,487
We found three thousand three hundred and twenty documents from a hundred

9
00:00:32,537 --> 00:00:36,554
and nineteen countries. I am going to tell you what they say,

10
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:40,621
and I am going to be honest about how much we can

11
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:44,688
trust what they say. You do not need any background in robotics,

12
00:00:44,738 --> 00:00:48,755
in AI or in law. I will give you ten headlines, and

13
00:00:48,805 --> 00:00:52,822
then I will tell you what I think they mean.

14
00:00:53,872 --> 00:00:58,685
Here is why the body matters. When a piece of software goes

15
00:00:58,735 --> 00:01:03,548
wrong, you get a bad sentence, a bad score, a bad recommendation.

16
00:01:03,598 --> 00:01:08,410
Serious things, but reversible. When an AI with a body goes wrong,

17
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:13,273
you get an event. A collision. A crushed hand. A fall in

18
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:18,136
a hallway at two in the morning. The questions change the moment

19
00:01:18,186 --> 00:01:22,999
the intelligence can move. Can it hurt someone? A two-hundred-kilogram robot arm

20
00:01:23,049 --> 00:01:27,861
can. A one-and-a-half-tonne car can. How close may it work to a

21
00:01:27,911 --> 00:01:32,724
person? Who pays when it breaks something, or someone? And the hardest

22
00:01:32,774 --> 00:01:37,587
one: how much may the machine decide on its own before a

23
00:01:37,637 --> 00:01:42,450
human has to step in? Those four questions are not the questions

24
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,312
in most AI ethics guidelines, which are about bias, privacy and transparency.

25
00:01:47,362 --> 00:01:52,175
So we asked something plain. Anywhere in the world, what rules exist

26
00:01:52,225 --> 00:01:57,038
for AI with a body, and what do they actually say?

27
00:01:58,088 --> 00:02:03,225
We built a map. Three thousand three hundred and twenty documents: laws,

28
00:02:03,275 --> 00:02:08,412
regulations, standards, strategies, guidelines, research papers and news. A hundred and nineteen

29
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:13,600
countries, the European Union and the international bodies. The collection took three

30
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:18,787
rounds. Regional teams searched the web first. Then AI agents searched the

31
00:02:18,837 --> 00:02:23,974
official legal databases, where the binding law actually lives, and found most

32
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:29,161
of it. Then a third round went back for the texts the

33
00:02:29,211 --> 00:02:34,348
databases had blocked. Every document was read by an AI model against

34
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:39,536
the same thirty-nine questions. What kind of document is this? Is it

35
00:02:39,586 --> 00:02:44,723
binding? Which machines does it cover? What does it say about safety,

36
00:02:44,773 --> 00:02:49,910
about control, about who is liable? Two things to hold in mind

37
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:55,097
for the rest of the talk. First, this is a map, not

38
00:02:55,147 --> 00:03:00,284
a census. Every count I give you is a floor. Second, no

39
00:03:00,334 --> 00:03:05,472
person read every document. The labels came from AI, were checked by

40
00:03:05,522 --> 00:03:10,659
a second AI, and were audited by an independent AI reader. Treat

41
00:03:10,709 --> 00:03:15,846
them as consistent, not certified. I will come back to that.

42
00:03:16,896 --> 00:03:21,557
Headline one. Robots are governed sideways. We found two hundred and seventy-eight

43
00:03:21,607 --> 00:03:26,269
binding rules in the world that reach robots: statutes, regulations and decrees

44
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:30,980
with legal force, each counted once, with their amendments but without their

45
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:35,692
paperwork. Of those two hundred and seventy-eight, twenty-five name artificial intelligence at

46
00:03:35,742 --> 00:03:40,403
all. Two hundred and one contain some provision about autonomy or automation.

47
00:03:40,453 --> 00:03:45,114
The rest are aviation law, road-traffic law, machinery law, product-safety law and

48
00:03:45,164 --> 00:03:49,826
medical-device law. They reach robots not because anyone sat down to write

49
00:03:49,876 --> 00:03:54,537
a robot law, but because a drone is legally an aircraft, a

50
00:03:54,587 --> 00:03:59,248
self-driving car is legally a vehicle, and a robot arm is legally

51
00:03:59,298 --> 00:04:03,960
a machine. Almost none of them were written with AI in mind.

52
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:08,671
If you came to this talk expecting a wave of robot regulation,

53
00:04:08,721 --> 00:04:13,383
this is the first surprise. The wave exists, but it is mostly

54
00:04:13,433 --> 00:04:18,094
old law catching new machines.

55
00:04:19,144 --> 00:04:24,489
Look at what those two hundred and seventy-eight rules are about. A

56
00:04:24,539 --> 00:04:29,884
hundred and forty-six address drones. A hundred and nine address self-driving road

57
00:04:29,934 --> 00:04:35,280
vehicles. Twenty-nine address sidewalk delivery robots, mostly American state statutes that define

58
00:04:35,330 --> 00:04:40,675
a personal delivery device and tell it how fast it may go.

59
00:04:40,725 --> 00:04:46,070
Then the numbers fall off a cliff. Fourteen for security and policing

60
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:51,465
robots. Thirteen for factory arms. Twelve for warehouse robots. Nine for farm

61
00:04:51,515 --> 00:04:56,860
machines. Four for care robots. Zero for companion robots. Zero for humanoids.

62
00:04:56,910 --> 00:05:02,256
The law reaches the machines that move through public space, where a

63
00:05:02,306 --> 00:05:07,651
collision is visible and a regulator already exists. It has not reached

64
00:05:07,701 --> 00:05:13,046
the machines that are about to move into private space.

65
00:05:14,096 --> 00:05:18,551
That is headline two, and it is the one I most want

66
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:23,057
you to remember. The robots we will live with have almost no

67
00:05:23,107 --> 00:05:27,562
rules. Zero binding rules for companion and social robots. Zero for humanoids.

68
00:05:27,612 --> 00:05:32,068
Four for care and assistive robots. Across the whole collection, four hundred

69
00:05:32,118 --> 00:05:36,573
and twenty-nine documents discuss those three kinds of machine. Four of them

70
00:05:36,623 --> 00:05:41,079
are binding law, and those four are general laws that mention care

71
00:05:41,129 --> 00:05:45,584
robots in passing: a Salvadoran robotics act, a Korean robot-industry act, and

72
00:05:45,634 --> 00:05:50,090
two Japanese special-zone ordinances. The one text written specifically for care robots

73
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:54,595
is a voluntary standard, ISO 13482, from 2014. That is the whole

74
00:05:54,645 --> 00:05:59,101
legal inheritance of the robot that will help your mother out of

75
00:05:59,151 --> 00:06:03,606
bed.

76
00:06:04,656 --> 00:06:09,890
Now put the research next to the rules. This chart shows what

77
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:15,174
share of five hundred and nine research papers, in blue, and what

78
00:06:15,224 --> 00:06:20,458
share of five hundred and ninety-eight governance instruments, in copper, address each

79
00:06:20,508 --> 00:06:25,742
kind of robot. Companion and social robots: forty-three percent of the research,

80
00:06:25,792 --> 00:06:31,026
three percent of the rules. Care robots: twenty-eight and nine. Humanoids: twenty-three

81
00:06:31,076 --> 00:06:36,310
and four. Now the bottom of the chart. Self-driving vehicles: twelve percent

82
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:41,594
of the research, forty-one percent of the rules. Drones: six and forty-three.

83
00:06:41,644 --> 00:06:46,878
Scientists study the robots people will live with. Lawmakers regulate the things

84
00:06:46,928 --> 00:06:52,162
that already move through streets and skies. We checked whether this gap

85
00:06:52,212 --> 00:06:57,446
was an artefact of where we searched, because the legal databases were

86
00:06:57,496 --> 00:07:02,730
queried for drones and vehicles. It is not. For companions, carers and

87
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:08,014
humanoids, the gap holds whichever way the instruments were collected.

88
00:07:09,064 --> 00:07:14,752
Headline three. The body changes the rulebook. We coded every one of

89
00:07:14,802 --> 00:07:20,490
the twenty values and concerns in these documents as absent, mentioned, or

90
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:26,228
spelled out in detail. Here is what the binding rules spell out.

91
00:07:26,278 --> 00:07:31,965
Physical safety: a hundred and sixty-seven of two hundred and seventy-eight in

92
00:07:32,015 --> 00:07:37,703
detail. Testing and certification before sale: a hundred and thirty-two. Who is

93
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:43,441
accountable: a hundred and thirty. Human oversight: eighty-three. The level of autonomy

94
00:07:43,491 --> 00:07:49,179
allowed: seventy-three. Liability for damage: sixty-three. Now the right-hand column, the vocabulary

95
00:07:49,229 --> 00:07:54,917
of AI ethics. Privacy in detail: forty-four. Transparency: eight. Fairness: three. Trust:

96
00:07:54,967 --> 00:08:00,655
three. Human dignity: one. Sustainability: none. This is not a criticism of

97
00:08:00,705 --> 00:08:06,392
the law. It is a description of it. When the intelligence has

98
00:08:06,442 --> 00:08:12,130
a body, the rulebook is about the body: can it hurt you,

99
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:17,868
was it tested, who answers for it. The values that dominate the

100
00:08:17,918 --> 00:08:23,606
AI ethics literature barely appear.

101
00:08:24,656 --> 00:08:29,144
Headline four. Mention is not detail. The control question, how much the

102
00:08:29,194 --> 00:08:33,682
machine may decide before a person must step in, is the question

103
00:08:33,732 --> 00:08:38,220
that makes a robot rule a robot rule. A hundred and seventy-one

104
00:08:38,270 --> 00:08:42,758
of the binding rules raise it. Seventy-three answer it, in the sense

105
00:08:42,808 --> 00:08:47,296
of saying what is allowed, by whom, and checked how. Human oversight:

106
00:08:47,346 --> 00:08:51,834
a hundred and seventy-four raise it, eighty-three answer it. So six rules

107
00:08:51,884 --> 00:08:56,372
in ten raise the question and fewer than three in ten answer

108
00:08:56,422 --> 00:09:00,910
it. And most of the answers are in aviation and road-vehicle law,

109
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:05,448
where words like remote pilot and fallback driver already have legal meaning.

110
00:09:05,498 --> 00:09:09,986
Everywhere else, the law gestures. It says there shall be appropriate human

111
00:09:10,036 --> 00:09:14,524
control, and it stops. That gap between mention and detail is where

112
00:09:14,574 --> 00:09:19,062
the next decade of litigation will live.

113
00:09:20,112 --> 00:09:24,980
Headline five is new in this version of the study. We went

114
00:09:25,030 --> 00:09:29,897
back to every rule and asked it a question the codebook had

115
00:09:29,947 --> 00:09:34,815
not asked: by your own words, who has to obey you? Sixty-three

116
00:09:34,865 --> 00:09:39,732
percent name public bodies. Sixty-two percent name operators and deployers, the companies

117
00:09:39,782 --> 00:09:44,650
that run the machine. Thirty-nine percent name manufacturers. Thirty-eight, owners and users.

118
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:49,568
Twenty-five, pilots and drivers. Eighteen percent name individuals, the general public, you.

119
00:09:49,618 --> 00:09:54,485
And eight percent, one rule in twelve, name the developers and providers

120
00:09:54,535 --> 00:09:59,403
of the software that does the deciding. The duty sits with whoever

121
00:09:59,453 --> 00:10:04,320
runs the machine in the world. The people who write the model

122
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:09,238
that steers it are almost nowhere in the law.

123
00:10:10,288 --> 00:10:15,470
Headline six. These rules are new, and they arrive after the machines.

124
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:20,702
Three quarters of the binding rules date from 2019 or later. The

125
00:10:20,752 --> 00:10:25,934
three years from 2024 to October 2026 alone account for a hundred

126
00:10:25,984 --> 00:10:31,166
and five of the two hundred and seventy-eight. Look at the pattern.

127
00:10:31,216 --> 00:10:36,398
Drone rules followed drones into the sky, mostly between 2016 and 2021,

128
00:10:36,448 --> 00:10:41,630
after the near-misses at airports. Self-driving rules followed the test fleets onto

129
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:46,862
the road. Delivery-robot statutes followed sidewalk robots into American cities between 2018

130
00:10:46,912 --> 00:10:52,094
and 2022. Humanoids and companion robots have now arrived, in showrooms, in

131
00:10:52,144 --> 00:10:57,326
pilot programmes, in national strategies. On this pattern, their rules will follow

132
00:10:57,376 --> 00:11:02,558
too, and on this pattern, they will follow the incidents. That is

133
00:11:02,608 --> 00:11:07,790
the part we can still change.

134
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:14,064
Headline seven. Three regions write almost all of it, and they write

135
00:11:14,114 --> 00:11:19,339
different things. North America has eighty-five of the binding rules, Europe eighty-two,

136
00:11:19,389 --> 00:11:24,613
Asia sixty-nine. Latin America sixteen, Oceania seven, Africa seven, the Middle East

137
00:11:24,663 --> 00:11:29,888
six. The United States legislates state by state: delivery robots in Pennsylvania,

138
00:11:29,938 --> 00:11:35,162
autonomous vehicles in Michigan, drones over prisons in a dozen places. The

139
00:11:35,212 --> 00:11:40,437
European Union regulates machines as products: the Machinery Regulation, the drone regulations,

140
00:11:40,487 --> 00:11:45,711
and the AI Act reaching robots as safety components. And Japan, China

141
00:11:45,761 --> 00:11:50,986
and Korea, with fifty-seven rules between them, are the only places on

142
00:11:51,036 --> 00:11:56,260
earth with laws that say robot in the title. Korea's Intelligent Robots

143
00:11:56,310 --> 00:12:01,535
Act. Japan's Level-4 amendments to its road-traffic law. Chinese city regulations for

144
00:12:01,585 --> 00:12:06,809
driverless cars, and the 2025 and 2026 plans for what Beijing and

145
00:12:06,859 --> 00:12:12,084
Shanghai call embodied intelligence. If you want to see what robot-specific law

146
00:12:12,134 --> 00:12:17,358
looks like, look east.

147
00:12:18,408 --> 00:12:23,765
Headline eight. The detailed safety knowledge is in standards, and standards are

148
00:12:23,815 --> 00:12:29,171
for sale. We found sixty-one technical standards in the collection, documents that

149
00:12:29,221 --> 00:12:34,578
say how to make a robot safe: ISO 10218 for factory arms,

150
00:12:34,628 --> 00:12:39,984
ISO 13482 for personal-care robots, ISO 3691-4 for driverless trucks, UL 4600

151
00:12:40,034 --> 00:12:45,391
for autonomous products. Forty-five declare themselves voluntary. One is referenced by a

152
00:12:45,441 --> 00:12:50,797
law, which makes it binding in effect. A law says be safe.

153
00:12:50,847 --> 00:12:56,204
The standard says how: how fast, how close, how much force, what

154
00:12:56,254 --> 00:13:01,610
to test. And fifty-seven of the sixty-one could only be read from

155
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:07,017
their public preview page, because the full text costs money. Nobody can

156
00:13:07,067 --> 00:13:12,423
read the standard without paying for it, and neither could we. The

157
00:13:12,473 --> 00:13:17,830
most practical safety knowledge about robots is the least public.

158
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:23,368
Headline nine, and this one is about us, and about every number

159
00:13:23,418 --> 00:13:27,906
you have ever seen on AI regulation. Where you look changes what

160
00:13:27,956 --> 00:13:32,444
you find. After round one, regional teams searching the web, we had

161
00:13:32,494 --> 00:13:36,982
five binding robot rules. After AI agents searched the legal databases, counting

162
00:13:37,032 --> 00:13:41,520
every legal text, two hundred and ninety-nine. After we removed the paperwork,

163
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:46,058
the commencement orders, the fee schedules, the index pages that databases serve

164
00:13:46,108 --> 00:13:50,596
up as separate entries, a hundred and ninety-five. After we recovered a

165
00:13:50,646 --> 00:13:55,134
hundred and eight texts the databases had blocked, two hundred and seventy-eight.

166
00:13:55,184 --> 00:13:59,672
Same world. Four counts. Every count of AI regulation you have seen

167
00:13:59,722 --> 00:14:04,210
depends on where someone looked and what they decided was one rule.

168
00:14:04,260 --> 00:14:08,748
Ours does too. The difference is that we report it, and you

169
00:14:08,798 --> 00:14:13,286
can rerun it.

170
00:14:14,336 --> 00:14:18,665
Headline ten. AI read the law about AI. How far can you

171
00:14:18,715 --> 00:14:23,043
trust it? Three numbers. Two different AI readers agreed with each other

172
00:14:23,093 --> 00:14:27,422
on a sample of a hundred and sixty documents at a kappa

173
00:14:27,471 --> 00:14:31,800
of point eight six; the same reader run twice agreed at point

174
00:14:31,850 --> 00:14:36,179
nine two. That is consistency. Then we took a random hundred of

175
00:14:36,229 --> 00:14:40,557
the binding rules and gave them to an independent AI auditor with

176
00:14:40,607 --> 00:14:44,936
three strict tests: is it a distinct instrument, is it binding, does

177
00:14:44,986 --> 00:14:49,314
it contain a substantive provision about a machine? Seventy-three passed, up from

178
00:14:49,364 --> 00:14:53,693
fifty-four a version earlier. What failed is instructive. Laws that mention a

179
00:14:53,743 --> 00:14:58,071
drone in a list of an agency's duties. Register pages captured instead

180
00:14:58,121 --> 00:15:02,450
of the statute. Two copies of the same EU regulation. The machine

181
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:06,828
reads consistently; it does not yet read like a lawyer. And the

182
00:15:06,878 --> 00:15:11,207
third number is zero: the number of documents a human has labelled

183
00:15:11,256 --> 00:15:15,585
so far. The validation kit for that is ready. Every figure in

184
00:15:15,635 --> 00:15:19,964
this talk is a well-measured estimate with an honest error bar. None

185
00:15:20,014 --> 00:15:24,342
of it is a fact.

186
00:15:25,392 --> 00:15:30,247
So what does it mean? Three things for the people in this

187
00:15:30,297 --> 00:15:35,152
room who do research. First, the research agenda and the legal agenda

188
00:15:35,202 --> 00:15:40,058
are inverted. Four papers in ten about robot governance are about companion

189
00:15:40,108 --> 00:15:44,963
robots. Three rules in a hundred are. All our evidence on human-robot

190
00:15:45,013 --> 00:15:49,868
interaction, on deception, on attachment, on dependency in care, has nowhere in

191
00:15:49,918 --> 00:15:54,773
law to land. Second, autonomy is a legal word now, and it

192
00:15:54,823 --> 00:15:59,679
is underspecified. A hundred and seventy-one rules invoke it; seventy-three define it.

193
00:15:59,729 --> 00:16:04,584
The researchers who can translate levels of autonomy into testable conditions are

194
00:16:04,634 --> 00:16:09,489
writing the next decade of law, whether they intend to or not.

195
00:16:09,539 --> 00:16:14,394
Third, reading law at scale is now possible, and it needs the

196
00:16:14,444 --> 00:16:19,300
same rigour as any instrument: blind reading, agreement between readers, independent audits,

197
00:16:19,350 --> 00:16:24,205
counting units stated in advance. Governance can be measured, not only argued.

198
00:16:24,255 --> 00:16:29,110
That is a methods agenda, and it is open.

199
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:35,226
And three moves for the people who make rules. One. Write the

200
00:16:35,276 --> 00:16:40,343
rules for the robots we will live with before the incidents, not

201
00:16:40,393 --> 00:16:45,459
after. Companions, carers and humanoids have zero, zero and four. Drones got

202
00:16:45,509 --> 00:16:50,575
their rules after the near-misses. The pattern is avoidable this time, because

203
00:16:50,625 --> 00:16:55,692
we can see it coming. Two. Answer the control question; do not

204
00:16:55,742 --> 00:17:00,808
just raise it. Who may let the machine decide what, under which

205
00:17:00,858 --> 00:17:05,925
conditions, checked by whom. Aviation law shows it can be done. The

206
00:17:05,975 --> 00:17:11,041
other sectors mostly gesture. Three. Make the safety knowledge public. When a

207
00:17:11,091 --> 00:17:16,157
law points to a standard, the standard becomes law in effect. Fifty-seven

208
00:17:16,207 --> 00:17:21,274
of our sixty-one standards are behind a paywall. Rules that people cannot

209
00:17:21,324 --> 00:17:26,390
read are rules that people cannot follow.

210
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:31,820
Before the takeaways, the edges of the map. Seventy-four official sources refused

211
00:17:31,870 --> 00:17:36,250
our crawler, mostly in France, Norway, Korea, Taiwan and the Chinese provinces.

212
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:40,680
Our crawler says who it is and does not pretend to be

213
00:17:40,730 --> 00:17:45,110
a browser, so those texts are listed for a person with ordinary

214
00:17:45,160 --> 00:17:49,540
access. Old statutes hide their robot provisions: a road-traffic act from 1960

215
00:17:49,590 --> 00:17:53,970
can carry a 2023 article on automated driving, and we could date

216
00:17:54,020 --> 00:17:58,400
the provision in only twenty-eight of the two hundred and seventy-eight rules,

217
00:17:58,450 --> 00:18:02,830
so our trend lines are floors, not histories. English is seventy-four percent

218
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:07,260
of the collection; we read Chinese, Japanese, Spanish and Portuguese in the

219
00:18:07,310 --> 00:18:11,690
original, but the search was richest in English and in the EU.

220
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:16,120
And no human has labelled a document. The next step in this

221
00:18:16,170 --> 00:18:20,550
study is a person, not a model.

222
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:27,074
Five things to take home. One. Robots are governed sideways: two hundred

223
00:18:27,124 --> 00:18:32,598
and seventy-eight binding rules reach them, twenty-five name AI, and the rest

224
00:18:32,648 --> 00:18:38,122
are aviation, road, machinery and product law. Two. The robots we will

225
00:18:38,172 --> 00:18:43,646
live with have almost none: companions zero, humanoids zero, care robots four,

226
00:18:43,696 --> 00:18:49,170
among four hundred and twenty-nine documents about them. Three. The body changes

227
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:54,694
the rulebook: physical safety, certification, accountability and liability lead; transparency, fairness and

228
00:18:54,744 --> 00:19:00,218
dignity barely appear. Four. Mention is not detail: six rules in ten

229
00:19:00,268 --> 00:19:05,742
raise the control question, fewer than three in ten answer it, and

230
00:19:05,792 --> 00:19:11,266
the duties fall on operators, almost never on the people who write

231
00:19:11,316 --> 00:19:16,790
the software. Five. Every count depends on where you looked. Ours went

232
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:22,314
five, two hundred and ninety-nine, a hundred and ninety-five, two hundred and

233
00:19:22,364 --> 00:19:27,838
seventy-eight. Ask any number about AI regulation how it was made.

234
00:19:28,888 --> 00:19:32,832
I will end where I began. The question is not whether robots

235
00:19:32,882 --> 00:19:36,825
will come into our homes. The strategies are written, the factories are

236
00:19:36,875 --> 00:19:40,819
tooling up, the demonstrations are on every feed. The question is whether

237
00:19:40,869 --> 00:19:44,812
the rules will be there when they do. Right now, for the

238
00:19:44,862 --> 00:19:48,806
machines that will share our kitchens and our care homes, the answer

239
00:19:48,856 --> 00:19:52,800
is no, and the gap is measured. The data, the code, every

240
00:19:52,850 --> 00:19:56,793
table and the list of what still needs a human are public,

241
00:19:56,843 --> 00:20:00,787
at the address on the screen, and you can explore all three

242
00:20:00,837 --> 00:20:04,780
thousand three hundred and twenty documents yourself. I would rather you check

243
00:20:04,830 --> 00:20:08,774
our numbers than believe them. Thank you.

